## Non-linear and partial-order planning

A **non-linear planner** may interleave steps belonging to different subgoals. The dominant technique is **partial-order planning (POP)**, which searches the space of *plans* rather than the space of *states*, and orders two actions only when it has a reason to.

A partial plan consists of:

- a set of **actions**, including two dummies: $\text{Start}$ (no preconditions, effects = the initial state) and $\text{Finish}$ (preconditions = the goal, no effects);
- **ordering constraints** $A \prec B$ ('A before B'), forming a partial order;
- **causal links** $A \xrightarrow{\,p\,} B$, meaning $A$ achieves precondition $p$ of $B$ and $p$ must remain true between them;
- a set of **open preconditions** not yet supported by any causal link.

The algorithm repeatedly picks an open precondition $p$ of some action $B$, chooses an action $A$ (existing or new) that has $p$ as an effect, adds the link $A \xrightarrow{p} B$ and the ordering $A \prec B$, and then resolves **threats**. An action $C$ **threatens** the link $A \xrightarrow{p} B$ if $C$ deletes $p$ and could be ordered between $A$ and $B$. Two repairs exist:

- **Demotion** — force $C \prec A$ (put the threat before the producer);
- **Promotion** — force $B \prec C$ (put the threat after the consumer).

The plan is complete when no open preconditions remain, and it is a solution if it is also **consistent** (the ordering constraints are acyclic). Any **linearisation** — any total order respecting the constraints — is then a valid plan, which is the great practical advantage: one partial-order plan represents many executable sequences, and the unordered actions can be executed in parallel or assigned to different agents.

On the Sussman anomaly, POP produces the 6-step plan directly. It adds $\text{STACK}(B,C)$ for $\text{ON}(B,C)$ and $\text{STACK}(A,B)$ for $\text{ON}(A,B)$ with no ordering between them at first; the threat arises because $\text{STACK}(A,B)$ deletes $\text{CLEAR}(B)$, which $\text{STACK}(B,C)$'s precondition chain needs, and promotion forces $\text{STACK}(B,C) \prec \text{STACK}(A,B)$ — the interleaving falls out of the threat resolution rather than being guessed.

Other non-linear planners named in the syllabus: **NOAH** (Sacerdoti, the first), **NONLIN**, **TWEAK**, **SNLP**, **UCPOP**, and the **constraint-posting** family generally. **GRAPHPLAN** builds a layered **planning graph** of alternating state and action levels with **mutex** relations, then extracts a plan by backward search; the planning graph also yields excellent admissible heuristics for forward search. **SATPLAN** encodes a bounded-length plan as a propositional formula and hands it to a SAT solver.

## The idea

A genetic algorithm is a **population-based stochastic search** method inspired by natural selection. Instead of improving one candidate solution, it maintains a population of them, scores each by a **fitness function**, preferentially breeds the better ones, and recombines and perturbs them to produce the next generation. Over many generations the population concentrates in high-fitness regions.

GAs belong to the family of **evolutionary computation** alongside evolution strategies, evolutionary programming and genetic programming (where the individuals are programs, usually trees, rather than fixed-length strings). In the search taxonomy of topic 10.1 a GA is **stochastic beam search with recombination**: it keeps $k$ states, but successors are produced by combining *pairs* of parents rather than by mutating one.

What a GA needs, and nothing more:
- a **representation (encoding)** of candidate solutions as chromosomes;
- a **fitness function** that scores a chromosome;
- **selection**, **crossover** and **mutation** operators;
- population size, crossover rate $p_c$, mutation rate $p_m$, and a **termination criterion**.

Notably it needs **no gradient, no derivative, and no continuity** — only the ability to score a candidate. That is why GAs are applied to combinatorial problems, to black-box optimisation, and to problems whose objective is discontinuous or noisy. The costs are that there is no optimality guarantee, the result varies between runs, and evaluating fitness for a whole population every generation is expensive.
